    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP, the priority today is maximizing efficiency while respecting the scarcity of stolen bases. **James Wood** stands out as the clear must-start with an elite 76.28 score; facing a vulnerable arm in Lorenzen with a +14% park factor and the leadoff spot makes him our primary engine for Runs and SB potential. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (69.99) and **Isaac Paredes** (68.21) also offer strong returns, as both benefit significantly from favorable matchups against right-handed pitching, providing the necessary stability for our 5x5 Roto push. We are also leaning into **Austin Riley**, whose score of 66.03 is bolstered by a wind-out advantage that projects to turn fly balls into HRs against Buehler.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Josh Naylor**, who sits at 52.67. While he remains a capable asset, he narrowly missed the cut behind the superior efficiency scores of **Gabriel Moreno** and **Max Muncy**, as the optimizer favored the specific platoon advantages of the latter. We are keeping a close eye on the 42% rain risk hovering over **Seiya Suzuki**; his 62.95 score is excellent, but should a delay or cancellation materialize, his value vanishes, forcing a potential pivot. Meanwhile, **Mickey Moniak** provides a marginal play at 45.72, though his high park factor and potential for SB on a slow pitcher delivery keep him in the fold despite the platoon disadvantage. We have effectively cleared the board of players like **Jose Caballero**, whose rainout makes him an easy exclusion, while **Edwin Arroyo** and **Cole Young** remain deep bench options as their projected contributions failed to surpass the starters currently active in our lineup.",
